	category="About"
>
	<p>
		<strong>WeirdJapan.news</strong> covers the strange, the small, and the slightly-too-much in
		Japanese culture &mdash; the stories most travel guides skip, the inventions that make foreign
		visitors stop and squint, the rituals that make perfect sense to Japan and zero sense to
		everyone else.
	</p>

	<h2>What we cover</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Only In Japan</strong> &mdash; vending machines that sell live crabs, cat islands, capsule hotels, themed cafés.</li>
		<li><strong>Tech &amp; quirks</strong> &mdash; bullet trains cleaned in 7 minutes, robot priests, toilets with 25 buttons.</li>
		<li><strong>Food</strong> &mdash; fugu chefs, ¥10,000 wagyu, Kit Kat flavors that shouldn't exist.</li>
		<li><strong>Culture &amp; tradition</strong> &mdash; tea ceremony, kintsugi, omikuji, the nightingale floors of Kyoto castles.</li>
		<li><strong>Lifestyle</strong> &mdash; the cultural mechanics behind hikikomori, inemuri, and the silent commuter train.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Who writes this</h2>
	<p>
		The site is edited by a small team based in Tokyo and produced with AI-assisted research and
		drafting. Every article is reviewed by a human editor before publication for factual accuracy,
		tone, and cultural sensitivity. We name our sources, we credit our images, and we correct what
		we get wrong.
	</p>

	<h2>How we make money</h2>
	<p>
		This site is reader-supported through <strong>affiliate links</strong>. When you book a hotel
		on Booking.com, a tour on Klook, or buy a recommended book on Amazon through a link on
		WeirdJapan.news, we may earn a small commission at no extra cost to you. This is how we keep
		the lights on.
	</p>
	<p>
		<em>We never accept payment for editorial coverage.</em> Brands and tourism boards cannot buy
		their way into our articles. Affiliate links are added to recommendations we'd make anyway, not
		the other way around.
	</p>

	<h2>Image credits</h2>
	<p>
		Hero images on most articles are sourced from <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a>
		under their respective free-use licenses, or from <a href="https://unsplash.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unsplash</a>
		under the Unsplash License. Each article credits the original photographer.
	</p>

	<h2>Contact &amp; corrections</h2>
	<p>
		Spotted a factual error? An out-of-date detail? A weird Japan story we should cover next? Send
		us a note at <a href="mailto:hi@weirdjapan.news">hi@weirdjapan.news</a>. We read everything.
	</p>

	<h2>Follow along</h2>
	<p>
		New oddities go up almost every day. The fastest way to keep up:
	</p>
